{
  "policy_id": "ld-118-tackle-asylum-backlog-and-allow-asylum",
  "party": "Liberal Democrat",
  "policy_title": "Tackle Asylum Backlog and Allow Asylum Seekers to Work",
  "measure": "D1",
  "verdict": {
    "measure": "D1",
    "shift": "toward more open",
    "net_migration": "unclear",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "This policy would speed up asylum decisions and let asylum seekers work sooner, opening up more access to the labour market. It also aims to speed up returns, which pulls slightly the other way, but experts say easier work access is unlikely to noticeably raise arrivals.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether faster processing and work rights act as a pull factor on arrivals is disputed, and the appeals backlog (now at record levels) may limit how much the initial-decision speed-up reduces overall system pressure.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy also commits to speeding up returns of those without a right to stay.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "speeding up returns of those without a right to stay"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Most asylum decisions in early 2025 took six months, so a three-month standard would be a significant acceleration.",
        "evidence_id": "E6",
        "quote": "achieving a three-month service standard for all but the most complex claims would represent a significant acceleration beyond current processing times, which saw most initial decisions taking six months in early 2025"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The asylum appeals backlog has nearly doubled to a record 80,000, with average wait of 67 weeks, limiting overall system relief.",
        "evidence_id": "E3",
        "quote": "the asylum appeals backlog has almost doubled in 2025, reaching a record 80,000 applications, with an average waiting time of 67 weeks to clear an appeal"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The proposed three-month work eligibility is a significant liberalisation compared with current rules and those of comparable countries.",
        "evidence_id": "E13",
        "quote": "The proposed policy to allow work after three months would be a significant liberalisation of the current rules, which are considered more restrictive than those in many comparable countries, including the EU, Canada, and the USA"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Experts including the Migration Advisory Committee find little evidence that labour market access significantly affects asylum arrival numbers.",
        "evidence_id": "E25",
        "quote": "experts, including the Migration Advisory Committee and analysis by UK in a Changing Europe, state that there is \"little evidence\" that labor market access has a significant impact on the number of arrivals"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Removals are constrained by non-cooperating home countries, limiting the returns element of the policy.",
        "evidence_id": "E10",
        "quote": "removals can be difficult if the individual's home country does not cooperate"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The policy moves in two directions simultaneously: faster processing and earlier work access open up the system (more routes, faster resolution, broader labour market participation), while the returns commitment points toward more control. On net, the work-rights change is the more novel and structurally significant shift \u2014 current rules are more restrictive than most comparable countries, so the three-month threshold represents a meaningful liberalisation. The returns component is directionally tightening but constrained by practical barriers. Because expert consensus finds little pull-factor effect from work rights, the net-migration impact is unclear rather than clearly raised. The appeals backlog at record levels is a significant caveat on how much any initial-decision speedup changes overall outcomes.",
